`FOR UPDATE` waits; `NOWAIT` fails fast with `55P03`; `SKIP LOCKED` silently skips. Use `SKIP LOCKED` for queues/pools, `NOWAIT` for “resource busy, try later” UX, and plain `FOR UPDATE` when you need strict ordering and can wait.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Row locking modes: FOR UPDATE (block) vs NOWAIT (error) vs SKIP LOCKED (skip)</text><rect x="30" y="35" width="250" height="110" rx="14" fill="none" stroke="#ef4444"/><text x="155" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#ef4444">FOR UPDATE (default)</text><text x="155" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">waits for lock to release</text><text x="155" y="96" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">blocks worker</text><text x="155" y="114" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">can deadlock if unordered</text><text x="155" y="132" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">timeout via lock_timeout</text><rect x="300" y="35" width="250" height="110" rx="14" fill="none" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="425" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">FOR UPDATE NOWAIT</text><text x="425" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">error immediately if locked</text><text x="425" y="96" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">55P03 lock_not_available</text><text x="425" y="114" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">app catches &amp; retries elsewhere</text><text x="425" y="132" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">good for user-facing “busy”</text><rect x="570" y="35" width="220" height="110" rx="14" fill="none" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="680" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</text><text x="680" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">silently skips locked rows</text><text x="680" y="96" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">never blocks, never errors</text><text x="680" y="114" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">perfect for queues / pools</text><text x="680" y="132" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">order by priority, id</text></svg></div>

| Mode | If row locked | Use case | Error |
|---|---|---|---|
| `FOR UPDATE` | Block until unlocked (or `lock_timeout`) | Bank transfer, inventory decrement (must wait) | Timeout if `lock_timeout` set |
| `FOR UPDATE NOWAIT` | Error immediately | Seat booking “someone else is holding it” | `55P03 lock_not_available` |
| `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` | Skip, return next free row | Job queue, connection pool, task dispatch | None (just fewer rows) |
| `FOR SHARE` | Shared lock | Read-mostly, allow concurrent reads | — |

> **Tip:** Set `SET lock_timeout = '2s'` with plain `FOR UPDATE` to avoid infinite waits; with `SKIP LOCKED` you rarely need a timeout. Always `ORDER BY id` when claiming to avoid deadlocks from unordered locking.

```sql
-- Seat booking: fail fast if someone else holds the seat (NOWAIT)
BEGIN;
SELECT * FROM seats WHERE id = 42 FOR UPDATE NOWAIT; -- throws 55P03 if locked
-- app catches 55P03 -> "Seat is being booked, try another"
UPDATE seats SET held_by = $1 WHERE id = 42;
COMMIT;

-- Queue: skip locked, claim next (SKIP LOCKED)
BEGIN;
SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE status='pending'
ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 5;
-- process & update to 'done'
COMMIT;

-- Strict order: wait (with timeout)
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '3s';
SELECT * FROM inventory WHERE sku='WIDGET' FOR UPDATE; -- waits up to 3s
UPDATE inventory SET qty = qty - 1 WHERE sku='WIDGET';
COMMIT;
```
